# How Git may and may not touch these files.
#
# Without this file Git guesses, file by file, whether something is text and
# therefore whether to rewrite its line endings on checkout.  The guess is
# usually right - it looks for a NUL byte in the first 8 KB - but "usually" is
# not good enough here.  This repository's whole point is byte-exact card
# images: a single CR inserted into one would break its CRC-16, the card would
# stop working, and the round trip would fail for whoever cloned it, with no
# obvious cause.  So the binaries are named explicitly.

# Text: stored with LF in the repository AND checked out with LF everywhere.
#
# `text=auto` alone would store LF and hand Windows CRLF, which is the usual
# arrangement and is harmless to every tool here - but it means a clone is not
# byte-identical to the repository, so checksums taken on one machine do not
# match another.  In a project where the whole discipline is byte-exactness,
# and where the maker scripts are supposed to reproduce their output exactly,
# that is a trap.  `eol=lf` gives every platform the same bytes.
#
# Nothing here minds: Python, C, Make and Git Bash all read LF natively on
# Windows, and so does every editor written this century.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Card images and everything else that is bytes rather than characters.
# -text means: never convert, never diff as text, never merge as text.
*.bin       -text
*.mid       -text
*.wav       -text
*.fmlog     -text
*.rom       -text
*.ROM       -text
*.jpg       -text
*.jpeg      -text
*.png       -text
*.gif       -text
*.exe       -text
*.zip       -text

# Things that must keep LF even in a Windows working tree, because the tools
# that read them do not tolerate CR.
Makefile        text eol=lf
*.mk            text eol=lf
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.tcl           text eol=lf

# Let GitHub count the language properly: the vendored cores are not ours.
csrc/z80.c      linguist-vendored
csrc/z80.h      linguist-vendored
csrc/ymfm/**    linguist-vendored
